  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: \nLess severe threat but still storm chances remain thru early\nnext week.\n\nWe may get a couple more chances for organized convection, one\nthis evening and another Thursday with models showing stronger\nwaves in the northwest flow during these times.\n\nHowever, the severe threat seems limited. A cluster of\nshowers/storms in the Ohio Valley will likely impact the WV area\nby later this afternoon into the evening. Better support for any\nsevere looks to stay west of our area, but cannot rule out a few\nstronger storms toward the Mountain Empire of VA.\n\nAs for flooding, not that concerned, but with the upstream\ncluster and recent rainfall letting the flood watch ride for\nnow. If storms do not transpire by this evening, we likely can\ncancel it early.\n\nLess coverage Thursday with limited upper support based on model\nconsensus, so just typical widely scattered activity mainly in\nthe afternoon. Another wave does arrive later Thursday night\ninto Friday and probability of measurable precip is a little\nhigher especially over the mountains of WV and adjacent\nAlleghanys of VA. Timing of this wave to that late in the night\nleads to overall lower severe threat. May have to watch for\nflooding again if storms train over the same area.\n\n\nThe overall pattern aloft remains the same but the ridge does\nbuild a little more northeastward by this weekend and nudge the\nmore organized convection further north. Another stronger\nshortwave kicks the ridge southward again by early next week so\ncould see another better chance of storm coverage. The flow\nappears to become more westerly aloft by Tue-Wed so fast moving\nsystems aloft with limited showers/thunderstorm coverage.\n\n\nTemperatures through the period will likely stay above normal\nexcept a brief drop to near normal this weekend.",
